WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.957
In this lesson you'll learn about the hidden

2
00:00:03.957 --> 00:00:07.428
layer of files on your computer, what lives in it,

3
00:00:07.428 --> 00:00:09.704
and why you need to know it is there.

4
00:00:09.704 --> 00:00:12.734
Almost every project and app keeps its settings,

5
00:00:12.734 --> 00:00:15.869
its history, its secrets, and now its AI

6
00:00:15.869 --> 00:00:18.446
instructions in files your computer hides,

7
00:00:18.446 --> 00:00:20.292
or tucks out of the way.

8
00:00:20.292 --> 00:00:22.255
Hidden only means out of sight.

9
00:00:22.255 --> 00:00:23.775
It never means locked.

10
00:00:23.775 --> 00:00:26.190
Your secret keys live in that layer.

11
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.199
And AI agents read and write it,

12
00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:29.789
so it's the first place to look

13
00:00:29.789 --> 00:00:32.647
when something changed that you didn't see.

14
00:00:33.097 --> 00:00:35.013
This lesson sits in District One,

15
00:00:35.013 --> 00:00:38.078
The Machine, which answers one question:

16
00:00:38.078 --> 00:00:40.040
what's on your computer?

17
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.105
The last lesson gave every file an address.

18
00:00:43.105 --> 00:00:44.846
This one shows you the addresses

19
00:00:44.846 --> 00:00:48.308
your computer leaves off the tour.

20
00:00:48.758 --> 00:00:51.080
Picture your computer as a shop.

21
00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.030
The shop floor is what File Explorer

22
00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:54.656
or Finder shows you:

23
00:00:54.656 --> 00:00:56.873
your documents and your code.

24
00:00:56.873 --> 00:00:59.161
The porter from two lessons back

25
00:00:59.161 --> 00:01:01.633
gives the tour of that floor.

26
00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:04.292
At the back, the porter walks straight past

27
00:01:04.292 --> 00:01:07.380
a door with a sign on it: staff only.

28
00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:08.773
Behind that door is everything

29
00:01:08.773 --> 00:01:10.433
that keeps the shop running.

30
00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:12.419
The settings. The history.

31
00:01:12.419 --> 00:01:14.520
The supplies. The keys.

32
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.587
You don't need any of it to shop.

33
00:01:16.587 --> 00:01:18.654
You need it the day something breaks.

34
00:01:18.654 --> 00:01:19.931
Keep this picture.

35
00:01:19.931 --> 00:01:21.755
The back room is the lesson.

36
00:01:22.205 --> 00:01:24.214
There are two kinds of sign.

37
00:01:24.214 --> 00:01:25.386
On Mac and Linux,

38
00:01:25.386 --> 00:01:27.836
the sign is painted into the name:

39
00:01:27.836 --> 00:01:30.587
a name that starts with a dot is skipped

40
00:01:30.587 --> 00:01:32.793
when a folder is listed.

41
00:01:32.793 --> 00:01:33.943
As Rob Pike,

42
00:01:33.943 --> 00:01:36.915
one of the early Unix programmers, tells it,

43
00:01:36.915 --> 00:01:40.049
this began as a shortcut in the list command,

44
00:01:40.049 --> 00:01:42.406
meant to hide two special entries,

45
00:01:42.406 --> 00:01:44.740
dot and dot dot.

46
00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:47.236
It hid every name that starts with a dot,

47
00:01:47.236 --> 00:01:49.941
and people started putting settings there.

48
00:01:49.941 --> 00:01:52.054
Pike later called it a mistake.

49
00:01:52.054 --> 00:01:54.167
Windows ignores the dot.

50
00:01:54.167 --> 00:01:57.348
There, hiding is a flag hung on the file itself,

51
00:01:57.348 --> 00:01:59.449
called the Hidden attribute.

52
00:01:59.449 --> 00:02:02.607
So on Windows, a dotted name sits in plain view

53
00:02:02.607 --> 00:02:04.639
unless something hung the flag.

54
00:02:04.639 --> 00:02:08.342
Git for Windows flags its own .git folder.

55
00:02:08.342 --> 00:02:10.676
Every system can show hidden items.

56
00:02:10.676 --> 00:02:12.301
The names are on screen.

57
00:02:12.301 --> 00:02:14.149
The steps can wait.

58
00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.655
There's more than one back room.

59
00:02:16.655 --> 00:02:19.128
Each project has its own: the dot folders

60
00:02:19.128 --> 00:02:22.019
and dot files inside the project folder.

61
00:02:22.019 --> 00:02:23.749
They travel with the project.

62
00:02:23.749 --> 00:02:26.430
And you have one at home, in your home folder,

63
00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:28.636
for settings that belong to you.

64
00:02:28.636 --> 00:02:31.829
On Linux, the tidy answer is a .config folder,

65
00:02:31.829 --> 00:02:34.418
and many tools use it on a Mac too.

66
00:02:34.418 --> 00:02:36.171
Plenty of tools ignore it and leave

67
00:02:36.171 --> 00:02:38.772
their own dot folder at the top instead,

68
00:02:38.772 --> 00:02:42.441
like .ssh or .claude.

69
00:02:42.441 --> 00:02:45.274
On Windows, the home back room is AppData,

70
00:02:45.274 --> 00:02:48.339
hidden on a normal install, with three rooms.

71
00:02:48.339 --> 00:02:50.080
Roaming is meant to follow a work

72
00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.274
login from machine to machine.

73
00:02:52.274 --> 00:02:54.794
Local stays on this machine.

74
00:02:54.794 --> 00:02:56.849
LocalLow is for programs running

75
00:02:56.849 --> 00:02:58.230
with fewer permissions;

76
00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:00.368
today it's mostly games.

77
00:03:00.818 --> 00:03:02.734
Now the project's shelves.

78
00:03:02.734 --> 00:03:04.243
The big one is the ledger:

79
00:03:04.243 --> 00:03:06.333
a folder called .git,

80
00:03:06.333 --> 00:03:09.096
holding every past version of your project.

81
00:03:09.096 --> 00:03:12.254
Delete that one folder and your files stay.

82
00:03:12.254 --> 00:03:14.529
Their history is gone.

83
00:03:14.529 --> 00:03:19.068
Next to it sits a private toolbox: .venv.

84
00:03:19.068 --> 00:03:20.880
It holds a pointer to your Python

85
00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.260
and the project's own packages.

86
00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:26.000
Aaron, who made this course, had an IT degree

87
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.471
and still didn't know what .venv was.

88
00:03:29.471 --> 00:03:33.290
Since Python 3.13, and in the tool uv,

89
00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.926
a new one tells git to ignore itself.

90
00:03:35.926 --> 00:03:39.060
Taped to the wall is a list: .gitignore.

91
00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.149
It names what never goes on the moving van.

92
00:03:42.149 --> 00:03:43.588
It hides nothing.

93
00:03:43.588 --> 00:03:46.584
It only tells git what not to pack.

94
00:03:46.584 --> 00:03:50.533
Editor settings live here too, like .vscode.

95
00:03:50.983 --> 00:03:53.375
Now the drawer that matters most.

96
00:03:53.375 --> 00:03:55.360
At the root of many projects sits

97
00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:58.321
a file called .env.

98
00:03:58.321 --> 00:04:02.187
Inside are plain lines: a name, an equals sign,

99
00:04:02.187 --> 00:04:06.006
and a value, usually a key or a password.

100
00:04:06.006 --> 00:04:07.562
When your program starts,

101
00:04:07.562 --> 00:04:09.733
if it's set up to look for this file,

102
00:04:09.733 --> 00:04:13.425
it loads those lines as environment variables.

103
00:04:13.425 --> 00:04:15.166
That drawer has no lock.

104
00:04:15.166 --> 00:04:16.652
It's plain text.

105
00:04:16.652 --> 00:04:19.810
Hidden means out of sight, not encrypted.

106
00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:23.723
Rule one: it never goes into git.

107
00:04:23.723 --> 00:04:25.453
Put it on the git ignore list

108
00:04:25.453 --> 00:04:27.218
before your first commit.

109
00:04:27.218 --> 00:04:29.075
Adding it later doesn't pull it out

110
00:04:29.075 --> 00:04:32.071
of the history, and once a key has leaked,

111
00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:34.787
the only real fix is to replace it.

112
00:04:34.787 --> 00:04:37.702
Rule two: never paste it into a chat,

113
00:04:37.702 --> 00:04:40.082
and never let an agent read it.

114
00:04:40.082 --> 00:04:42.497
Claude Code's own documentation says

115
00:04:42.497 --> 00:04:44.470
that anything a tool reads,

116
00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:47.257
a key included, ends up in a transcript

117
00:04:47.257 --> 00:04:49.799
file in another hidden folder.

118
00:04:49.799 --> 00:04:52.145
GitHub now blocks some known kinds of key

119
00:04:52.145 --> 00:04:54.432
from reaching public repositories,

120
00:04:54.432 --> 00:04:56.487
but only the kinds it recognises,

121
00:04:56.487 --> 00:04:58.089
and you can override it.

122
00:04:58.089 --> 00:05:00.622
It's a net, not a plan.

123
00:05:01.072 --> 00:05:03.197
Here's where the shop picture breaks.

124
00:05:03.197 --> 00:05:06.947
A real staff only sign is a rule people respect.

125
00:05:06.947 --> 00:05:11.242
A dot, or a Hidden flag, is only a display choice.

126
00:05:11.242 --> 00:05:14.261
Every program, every script, every agent,

127
00:05:14.261 --> 00:05:17.733
and every piece of malware walks straight in.

128
00:05:17.733 --> 00:05:19.126
There's no door, really.

129
00:05:19.126 --> 00:05:21.111
Just a tour that skips it.

130
00:05:21.111 --> 00:05:23.154
Real protection is something else:

131
00:05:23.154 --> 00:05:27.253
file permissions, encryption, a secrets manager.

132
00:05:27.253 --> 00:05:29.797
Those have lessons of their own.

133
00:05:30.247 --> 00:05:33.161
Now new staff have arrived, carrying lanterns:

134
00:05:33.161 --> 00:05:34.705
the AI agents.

135
00:05:34.705 --> 00:05:36.656
Before work, they read the notes pinned

136
00:05:36.656 --> 00:05:39.698
on the back-room wall, the instruction files:

137
00:05:39.698 --> 00:05:43.714
CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md,

138
00:05:43.714 --> 00:05:47.429
Cursor's rules, and Copilot's instructions.

139
00:05:47.429 --> 00:05:50.007
Next to them hangs a wiring board:

140
00:05:50.007 --> 00:05:54.744
MCP config files, like .mcp.json.

141
00:05:54.744 --> 00:05:57.437
They say which tools the staff may plug into.

142
00:05:57.437 --> 00:05:59.016
The staff also keep notebooks

143
00:05:59.016 --> 00:06:02.882
in your home back room: .claude and .codex.

144
00:06:02.882 --> 00:06:05.436
Skills live there, and so do notes

145
00:06:05.436 --> 00:06:07.898
Claude writes about you by itself.

146
00:06:07.898 --> 00:06:10.638
And the tools now read each other's notes.

147
00:06:10.638 --> 00:06:14.608
GitHub's Copilot cloud agent reads CLAUDE.md.

148
00:06:14.608 --> 00:06:18.184
Since September 2026, Claude Code reads

149
00:06:18.184 --> 00:06:22.642
AGENTS.md when a project has no CLAUDE.md.

150
00:06:22.642 --> 00:06:27.843
Since December 2025, AGENTS.md and MCP both

151
00:06:27.843 --> 00:06:30.397
belong to a Linux Foundation group.

152
00:06:30.397 --> 00:06:33.801
Each tool still keeps its own folder.

153
00:06:34.251 --> 00:06:37.479
In 2021, the back room held notes for tools:

154
00:06:37.479 --> 00:06:40.880
settings, history, a list of things to ignore.

155
00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:44.468
The worst common mistake was committing a .env.

156
00:06:44.468 --> 00:06:48.288
Since 2025, some of those notes are orders.

157
00:06:48.288 --> 00:06:49.762
An agent follows them,

158
00:06:49.762 --> 00:06:51.783
and some are commands it runs.

159
00:06:51.783 --> 00:06:53.187
So a stranger's note pinned

160
00:06:53.187 --> 00:06:55.567
to your wall gets obeyed too.

161
00:06:55.567 --> 00:06:58.493
In March 2025, researchers hid

162
00:06:58.493 --> 00:07:01.396
invisible instructions in a rules file.

163
00:07:01.396 --> 00:07:03.764
Others showed that a repository's

164
00:07:03.764 --> 00:07:05.773
own Claude settings could run commands

165
00:07:05.773 --> 00:07:08.118
or leak a key when someone opened it.

166
00:07:08.118 --> 00:07:10.777
Those holes were fixed, but the lesson stuck:

167
00:07:10.777 --> 00:07:12.658
these files are code.

168
00:07:12.658 --> 00:07:14.933
Malware learned the layout too.

169
00:07:14.933 --> 00:07:16.675
In August 2025,

170
00:07:16.675 --> 00:07:19.357
a poisoned package ran its victims' own AI

171
00:07:19.357 --> 00:07:23.141
command-line tools to search their disks for keys.

172
00:07:23.141 --> 00:07:26.833
GitGuardian, a company that scans for leaked keys,

173
00:07:26.833 --> 00:07:29.364
counted AI-service keys leaking about eighty

174
00:07:29.364 --> 00:07:34.091
percent more in 2025 than the year before.

175
00:07:34.541 --> 00:07:36.782
Your job is the owner's job.

176
00:07:36.782 --> 00:07:38.140
When an agent finishes,

177
00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:40.938
walk into the back room and look at what moved,

178
00:07:40.938 --> 00:07:43.040
hidden files included.

179
00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:45.373
Inside a project, git shows changes

180
00:07:45.373 --> 00:07:47.985
to dot files like any other file:

181
00:07:47.985 --> 00:07:49.866
git status lists them,

182
00:07:49.866 --> 00:07:51.689
and git diff shows the lines.

183
00:07:51.689 --> 00:07:53.059
Ignored files and your home

184
00:07:53.059 --> 00:07:54.975
back room don't show up there.

185
00:07:54.975 --> 00:07:58.121
A change to a rules file or an MCP config

186
00:07:58.121 --> 00:08:00.942
deserves the same review as a change to code.

187
00:08:00.942 --> 00:08:02.835
A note asking the agent not to read

188
00:08:02.835 --> 00:08:05.737
your keys is a request, not a lock.

189
00:08:05.737 --> 00:08:08.918
Tools offer real deny rules and sandboxes,

190
00:08:08.918 --> 00:08:10.695
and some agents now refuse

191
00:08:10.695 --> 00:08:13.111
to rewrite their own settings files.

192
00:08:13.574 --> 00:08:17.440
One password manager, 1Password, is testing,

193
00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:21.713
in beta, a fake .env that never touches the disk.

194
00:08:21.713 --> 00:08:24.118
The plain file is still the default.

195
00:08:24.567 --> 00:08:25.972
You'll open a project,

196
00:08:25.972 --> 00:08:29.025
see no .git folder, and git works anyway.

197
00:08:29.025 --> 00:08:30.850
It's there, just hidden.

198
00:08:31.312 --> 00:08:35.469
On Windows, .env and .ssh sit right

199
00:08:35.469 --> 00:08:38.464
there in Explorer, while .git doesn't.

200
00:08:38.464 --> 00:08:40.682
That's the flag, not the dot.

201
00:08:40.682 --> 00:08:43.816
You'll add .env to the ignore list,

202
00:08:43.816 --> 00:08:45.337
and it's still on GitHub.

203
00:08:45.337 --> 00:08:46.963
It was already committed.

204
00:08:46.963 --> 00:08:48.484
Replace the key.

205
00:08:48.484 --> 00:08:51.595
A push gets declined because it contains a secret.

206
00:08:51.595 --> 00:08:53.174
That's push protection.

207
00:08:53.174 --> 00:08:55.670
A setup guide for a desktop AI app sends

208
00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:59.013
you to a path with AppData\Roaming in it.

209
00:08:59.013 --> 00:09:00.708
That's your home back room.

210
00:09:00.708 --> 00:09:03.356
On a Mac, files called .DS_Store

211
00:09:03.356 --> 00:09:05.585
turn up in projects and commits.

212
00:09:05.585 --> 00:09:07.454
Finder leaves them behind.

213
00:09:07.454 --> 00:09:09.416
And dot folders linger from tools

214
00:09:09.416 --> 00:09:11.982
you stopped using, like the one an AI tool

215
00:09:11.982 --> 00:09:14.747
you tried once left in your home folder.

216
00:09:15.197 --> 00:09:16.892
Safe to ignore for now:

217
00:09:16.892 --> 00:09:20.863
the other folders next to .config, like .cache.

218
00:09:20.863 --> 00:09:23.231
The hidden Library folder on a Mac.

219
00:09:23.231 --> 00:09:26.319
Which settings file wins when two disagree.

220
00:09:26.319 --> 00:09:28.142
And dotfiles repos,

221
00:09:28.142 --> 00:09:30.510
for syncing your settings between machines.

222
00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:31.799
Recognise the names.

223
00:09:31.799 --> 00:09:33.565
Skip the details.

224
00:09:34.016 --> 00:09:37.278
The variables .env fills are module one,

225
00:09:37.278 --> 00:09:38.532
lesson five.

226
00:09:38.532 --> 00:09:40.866
Virtual environments are module five,

227
00:09:40.866 --> 00:09:42.236
lesson three.

228
00:09:42.236 --> 00:09:45.394
.gitignore and secrets are module eight,

229
00:09:45.394 --> 00:09:46.706
lesson seven.

230
00:09:46.706 --> 00:09:50.456
Instruction files are module three, lesson nine,

231
00:09:50.456 --> 00:09:55.088
and MCP config files are module ten, lesson nine.

232
00:09:55.088 --> 00:09:57.050
Keeping secrets out of plain text

233
00:09:57.050 --> 00:10:00.419
is module fifteen, lesson seven.

234
00:10:00.869 --> 00:10:03.179
Keep this. One:

235
00:10:03.179 --> 00:10:06.140
hidden means out of sight, not locked.

236
00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:08.915
A dot on Mac and Linux, a flag on Windows,

237
00:10:08.915 --> 00:10:10.878
and anything can still read it.

238
00:10:11.295 --> 00:10:14.917
Two: your secrets live in the hidden layer.

239
00:10:14.917 --> 00:10:17.378
.env is plain text.

240
00:10:17.378 --> 00:10:19.747
Never commit it, never paste it,

241
00:10:19.747 --> 00:10:21.964
and replace a key that leaked.

242
00:10:21.964 --> 00:10:25.018
Three: agents read and write this layer.

243
00:10:25.018 --> 00:10:27.746
After an agent works, check what changed,

244
00:10:27.746 --> 00:10:29.640
hidden files included.
